Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), assembled from organic ligands and metal ions or clusters, are booming as a class of fascinating crystalline materials. They feature well-defined pore geometry, high surface area and diverse framework composition. In virtue of these merits, MOFs hold great promising in a variety of potential applications.[1] However, most of the reported MOFs are restricted to the microporous regime. The small pore size would slow down mass transfer and prevent large guest molecules from accessing the active sites inside a host MOF.[2] Therefore, construction of MOFs with larger interconnected pores are indispensable for the applications with bulky molecules involved.
A few efforts have been devoted to build mesoporous MOFs (mesoMOFs). Pore channel expansion using elongated ligands was widely adopted, but instability upon solvent removal and interpenetration of the structures were almost unavoidable.[3] Defect-formation strategy has been also proposed to create large voids, unfortunately, the uneven void size and random void arrangement might still restrict mass transfer.[4] Template method is frequently utilized for the preparation of mesoporous silica since it could facilely control over the geometry of the produced mesopore.[5] It was recently extended for the construction of mesoMOFs and gained some encouraging achievements.[6] However, the instable pore structure of MOFs might be subject to collapse upon removing the template agent.[6i,7] Additionally, the template agent occasionally interferes with the formation of MOFs crystal, which might cause partial amorphization of mesoporous framework.[7] Keeping these pros and cons in mind, we intend to develop a rational surfactant self-assembly strategy to inherit the merits of template approach while to avoid the issues mentioned above.
Herein, we demonstrate our idea to elaborate such kind of mesoMOFs, which possess uniform mesochannels and wellcrystallized microporous framework with geometry complementary to that of surfactant self-assemblies. Additionally, the dimensions of continuous mesopores could be facilely tailored by changing the alkyl chain length of the applied surfactants. Furthermore, the obtained mesoMOFs exhibit exceptional stability so that the included surfactant could be effectively extracted upon the treatment with acidic solution.
The key design element in our strategy is the utilization of amphoteric surfactant, viz. cocamidopropyl betaine (CAPB) or oleyl amidopropyl betaine (OAPB), as a template to construct mesoMOFs. Consensus has achieved that the fine interaction between MOF precursor and surfactant is critical for the successful growth of mesoMOFs directed by the latter.[6g-h] The weak interaction would make ligands and metallic salts crystallize preferentially despite the surfactants. Amphoteric surfactant has the carboxylate functionality and quaternary ammonium as the hydrophilic head group (Scheme 1). The carboxylate functionality permits its strong bonding with metal clusters in MOFs, meanwhile, the quaternary ammonium group imparts high solubility for the otherwise insoluble fatty acid.[8] Theoretically, the bifunctional characteristic makes the amphoteric surfactant not only serve as a template but also a coordinating agent chemically attaching to the metal precursor during the self-assembling process, which would effectively avoid the phase separation of mesopore-free MOFs from surfactant assemblies.
Another important design element is that the reaction proceeds in a water-based system, which guarantees the formation of rod-shaped surfactant micelle to direct the growth of mesoMOFs. Actually, due to their amphipathy, it is difficult for most surfactants to form micelles in organic solvents, which are normally acted as the synthesis media for MOFs. Aqueous phase synthesis could effectively circumvent the issue that the randomly distributed mesopore was generated by the physical occupation of the individual surfactant molecule rather than their cylindrical assembly.[4d,6a-b] Furthermore, given the difficulty to remove surfactant without damage to the crystalline structure, the stability of matrix MOFs is of paramount importance. Therefore, our attention was paid to Zr-based UiO-66-type MOFs due to their exceptional stability,[9] as well as the fact that they could be indeed prepared from aqueous route as ongoing researches revealed.[10]
The UiO-66 type mesoMOFs (mesoUiO-66-X) was synthesized in aqueous solution at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Typically, $Z r{\mathsf{O C l}}_{2}{\cdot}8{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ and 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(B D C-N H_{2})$ ) were employed as inorganic precursor and organic ligand, respectively. CAPB was utilized as the template with appropriate amount of formic acid (FA) as a modulator. The pristine microporous UiO-66-NH2 was also prepared for comparison (Figure 1). The small-angle XRD (SAXRD) pattern of the synthesized sample (Figure 1A, b) displays a single diffraction peak at $2\theta\:=\:1.88^{\circ}$ and a broad shoulder at ${\sim}3.5^{\circ}$ . Similar single peak diffraction with broadened higher order reflections has been previously observed in hexagonal mesoporous HMS and MSU.[11] As expected, no smallangle peak is present for the pristine ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-66-N H}}_{2}$ since it possesses no mesopore (Figure 1A, a). The sharp and intense diffraction peaks in wide-angle XRD (WAXRD) pattern (Figure 1B, b) match well with those of microporous $U_{1}0\cdot66\cdot N H_{2}$ (Figure 1B, a),[9c] evidencing the crystalline nature of the mesopore walls. The sorption isotherm is identified as a combination mode of type I and type IV, indicative of the simultaneous existence of micropore and mesopore (Figure 1C, b and section S5.1 of the Supporting Information). A well-defined $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ condensation step at partial pressure of 0.2 to 0.4 discloses the presence of cylindrical mesopores with an average BJH pore size of about $2.8\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 1D, b and section S5.1 of the Supporting Information). The BET specific surface area is calculated to be $1410~\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , of which the micropore surface area is approximate $458~\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The single-point total volume of pores at $\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}=0.99$ is $1.28\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ .
Considering such a fact that the mesopore size of the obtained mesoMOFs is consistent with that of surfactant micelles, we suppose that surfactant with longer alkyl chain would afford larger mesopore. Thus, another amphoteric surfactant of OAPB with longer alkyl chain was used to template mesoUiO-66-NH2. In the presence of OAPB with optimal amount of FA modulator, larger mesopore is produced as expected. Additionally, the mesostructure features long-range hexagonal order. The longer alkyl chain makes the tail of betaine surfactant more hydrophobic, which would facilitate the formation of longer and relatively more “unbreakable” wormlike micelles,[12] assumedly benefitting to the formation of more ordered hexagonal phase. Three diffraction peaks in the SAXRD pattern are identified, which could be indexed to (100), (110) and (200) reflections of a typical 2D hexagonal $(p6m m)$ phase (Figure 1A, c). The main peak presents at $2\theta=1.78^{\circ}$ , which shifts to lower angle as compared to CAPBtemplated sample, corresponding to an increase in the pore-pore correlation. Identical conclusion is obtained from the ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm (Figure 1C, c and section S5.1 of the Supporting Information). A well-defined step at relative pressure of 0.3-0.5, associated with capillary condensation of ${\sf N}_{2}$ within mesopores, shifts to higher relative pressure due to the pore expansion. Correspondingly, the average BJH pore size increases to $3.2{\mathsf{n m}}$ (Figure 1D, c and section S5.1 of the Supporting Information). The BET surface area is calculated to be 1226 $\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ with a pore volume reaching $1.58~\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The sample contains a micropore surface area of 408 $\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , suggesting the existence of microporous frameworks in good agreement with the WAXRD pattern (Figure 1B, c).
Figure 2 shows the representative TEM images of the synthesized mesoMOFs. For CAPB-templated mesoUiO-66-NH2 (Figures 2a, S5), uniform mesopore with disordered worm-like arrangement can be observed. Magnified image depicts that the cylindrical mesopores are regular in diameter although they lack long-range packing order (Figure 2b). This microstructure is essentially analogous to mesoporous HMS or MSU in line with the deduction from SAXRD.[11] For the OAPB-templated sample, the hexagonal ordered mesopore could be clearly identified. Viewed parallel and perpendicular to the channel direction, the sample displays the characteristic honeycomb-like arrangement (Figures 2c, S6) and well-oriented parallel pore channels (Figure 2d), respectively. These studies further confirm the fact that the longrange periodicity has been evolved for the synthesized mesoMOFs in consistence with SAXRD.
It is worth noting that the amount of modulator plays a crucial role in the formation of mesoMOFs. If no or trace of FA is applied in the synthetic system, zirconium salt would hydrolyze and condense rapidly. The preferential coordination between carboxylate containing surfactant and zirconium precursor would produce the amorphous mesoporous zirconium hydrate.[8] As a result, no zirconium source is available for the construction of crystalline MOFs. The tentative experiment verified our proposal. When we simply mixed dicarboxylate ligand, zirconium salt and surfactant together, only an unidentified mesoporous phase was obtained. Correspondingly, a single diffraction peak presented at around $2\theta=2.2^{\circ}$ and $2.1^{\circ}$ for CAPB (Figure S7a) and OAPB (Figure S8a) templated systems, respectively, while no wide angle diffraction peaks of crystalline phase were detectable. It is commonly known that the monocarboxylic acid modulator can compete with multidentate linkers to control over the rate of nucleation and crystal growth.[13] Here, the suitable amount of FA not only protects Zr salt from the rapid hydrolysis and condensation but also adjusts the coordination interaction between metal nodes and organic ligands. For a given amount of surfactant, with the increase of FA, a broad single low-angle diffraction peak and characteristic wide-angle diffraction peaks of MOFs began to appear simultaneously in the XRD patterns (Figures S7b and 8b). As shown in Scheme 2, because the Zroxo clusters are saturated by FA on their periphery, the formation of mesoporous zirconium hydrate is inhibited, providing the possibility of constructing mesoMOFs. When optimized amount of FA was introduced, the microporous crystalline would grow along the anchored Zr layer at the periphery of cylindrical micelles and ordered mesopore would be created by hexagonal phase of surfactant assembly (Scheme 2). The optimal orderings were obtained with the FA/surfactant/Zr ratios of 12.12/0.438/1 and $22.35/0.80/1$ for CAPB and OAPB templated mesoUiO-66-NH2, respectively (see section S5.3 of the Supporting Information). However, an excessive amount of FA would tilt the subtle balance between anchoring and crystallization process, weakening the connection between surfactant and zirconium precursor. The microporous MOFs would crystallize independent of surfactant assembly, thereby, the resultant MOFs present poor ordered mesostructure (Figures S7d and 8d), or even no mesostructure (Figures S7e and 8e). Moreover, to verify the structural directing effect of the surfactants unambiguously, synthesis experiments with the optimal amount of FA and no surfactant were also conducted. As expected, no ${\mathsf{d}}_{100}$ peaks could be obtained in SAXRD patterns (see section S5.4 of the Supporting Information).
To further validate the reproducibility of this strategy, another water-soluble dicarboxylate ligand of 2,5- dihydroxyterephthalicacid $(B D C-(O H)_{2})$ was employed to synthesize mesoUiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ with CAPB and OAPB as templates. Similar results to the mesoUiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ were achieved for mesoUiO-66- $(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ . SAXRD pattern for mesoUiO-66- $(O H)_{2}$ templated with CAPB exhibits a broad diffraction peak with $2\theta=$ $1.96^{\circ}$ (Figure S15) while the crystalline nature of mesopore wall is confirmed by the well-resolved wide angle diffraction peaks (Figure S16), The presence of mesopore is supported by the steep capillary condensation at ${\sf P}/{\sf P}_{0}$ of 0.25 to 0.45 in ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm (Figure S3a). The mesopore size is determined to be approximate $2.6\:\:\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure S3c). Disordered hexagonal microstructure could be directly visualized by TEM image (Figure S17). For the OAPB-templated sample, XRD pattern exhibits three diffraction peaks of the typical hexagonal (p6mm) phase at small-angle range and characteristic peaks of microporous UiO$66-(O H)_{2}$ at wide-angle range (Figure 3a). ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption analysis reveals a BET surface area of $940m^{2}{\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ (the micropore surface is $287\ \mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1},$ ), a pore volume of $1.13\ c m^{3}{\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ and an average mesopore size of $3.0\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 3b and section S5.1 of the Supporting Information). The pendent dihydroxyl might occupy more space and consequently reduces the surface area and pore volume as compared to mesoUiO- $66\substack{-N H_{2}}$ .[9c-d,10b] The ordered hexagonal structure is also identified by TEM images (Figures 3c, 3d and S18).
FTIR spectra were recorded to track the template removal from the mesoMOFs (Figures S19-22). The strong IR absorption at $2800{\cdot}2950\ \mathsf{c m}^{\cdot1}$ , assigned to the characteristic CH vibration bands in amphoteric surfactant, can be clearly observed for the as-synthesized samples. After extraction, these peaks are almost undetectable. The signals at $\sim1655\ c m^{-1}$ , attributed to the $v(C=0)$ absorptions of the amide group in surfactants, are significantly weakened, indicative of the effective removal of template agents. Meanwhile, quantitative analysis of the efficiency of extraction procedure has been made through the liquid $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopy. It was found that almost all the surfactant has been removed from the extracted samples (see section S5.7.2 of the Supporting Information). TGA-DSC analysis was performed to further evaluate the extraction procedure (see section S5.7.3 of the Supporting Information). It shows that the decomposition temperature of the samples is not affected by extraction procedure, indicating that the framework crystallinity is maintained. This result is consistent with previous reports that HCl treatment has little effect on the $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ -ligand bonds but can effectively hydrolyze the $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ -monocarboxylate bonds.[4d,14] The good chemical stabilities of mesoUiO-66-NH2 and mesoUiO$66-(O H)_{2}$ were verified by the almost unaltered WAXRD patterns and textural parameters after their being maintained in solvents like DMF, acetone, benzene and water at room temperature for $24\ h$ in comparison to the as-synthesized samples (see section S5.8 of the Supporting Information).
To explore the accessibility of mesopore, cytochrome c (Cyt c, $2.6\times3.2\times3.3\mathsf{n m}^{3},$ encapsulation was tested on the resultant mesoMOFs.[15] As a control, microporous UiO-66-NH2 and UiO$66-(O H)_{2}$ were also prepared for comparison, of which the micropore sizes were reported to be around $7{\sim}9\mathrm{~\AA}$ and $6{\sim}8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively.[10b] (Figures S39-43). It can be directly visualized that supernatants for microporous crystalline still preserve red while those for mesoMOFs change to light red after Cyt c loading (Insets in Figures 4 and S44). This qualitatively illustrates that the mesoMOFs have a significantly higher accommodation capacity for Cyt c than microporous counterpart. As quantitatively determined from UV-Vis spectra, the amount of encapsulated Cyt c is as low as $34~\mathsf{m g}$ per $1.0\ g\ U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}$ , corresponding to the slightly reduced absorbance (Figure 4a). Meanwhile, the loading capacity increases quickly upon the introduction of mesopore, which is correlated with their mesopore sizes and pore volumes (Figures 4 and S44, Table S3). It reaches a maximum of 234 $\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{g}$ for OAPB-templated mesoUiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ . The effects of mesopore on the encapsulation of Cyt c were further investigated by ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption technique (Table S3, Figure S45). For microporous MOFs, the pore volumes reduce negligibly since Cyt c is too large to enter their micropore. For mesoMOFs, the reduction of pore volumes and specific surface area is dramatic and is mesopore-size dependent, in line with the UV-Vis measurement. It should be noted that the mesopore almost disappears while micropore is still remained to some extent after Cyt c encapsulation (Figure S45, Table S3), which verifies that the mesopore is fully accessible and plays a major role for Cyt c loading. Moreover, after Cyt c loading, the framework integrities of these MOFs are maintained as evidenced by PXRD studies (Figures S46-50).
In summary, a facile aqueous phase template strategy has been developed to prepare hierarchical micro- and mesoporous Zr-based MOFs. Amphoteric surfactant not only produces a hexagonal cylindrical mesophase but also offers a coordinating agent chemically anchoring to the metal precursor for the directed crystallization of MOFs along surfactant assemblies. The resultant mesoMOFs feature outstanding stability and tunable dimension of mesopores. The created uniform mesochannels are surrounded by the well-crystallized microporous framework and they could greatly enhance the accessibility of microspace in MOFs. The primary protein encapsulation experiment exemplifies the potential applications of the obtained mesoMOFs with bulky molecules involved. Extension of this method might pave the way for the preparation of a wide range of mesoMOFs, and work in this direction is in progress.
